-HTTPX will use universal line endings, normalising all cases to `\n`.
+By default, `iter_lines()` and `iter_lines(keepends=False)` remove line endings,
+using the same line boundaries as Python's `str.splitlines()`. Pass `keepends=True`
+to preserve the original endings, matching `str.splitlines(keepends=True)` on the
+decoded text. This includes `\r\n`, `\r`, `\n`, and all other separators recognized
+by `str.splitlines()`.
+
+```pycon
+>>> r = httpx.Response(200, text="one\r\ntwo\rlast")
+>>> list(r.iter_lines())
+['one', 'two', 'last']
+>>> list(r.iter_lines(keepends=True))
+['one\r\n', 'two\r', 'last']
+```
+
+This works for both streamed and already-read responses. Streaming remains
+incremental, including when a line ending spans chunks. Empty responses produce
+no lines, and a final line without a line ending is returned unchanged.
 
 In some cases you might want to access the raw bytes on the response without applying any HTTP content decoding. In this case any content encoding that the web server has applied such as `gzip`, `deflate`, `brotli`, or `zstd` will
 not be automatically decoded.
